.TH DRAND48_R 3
.SH NAME
drand48_r, erand48_r, lrand48_r, nrand48_r, mrand48_r, jrand48_r, srand48_r,
seed48_r, lcong48_r \- reentrant SVID 48-bit pseudo-random number generator
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.BI "int drand48_r(struct drand48_data *" buffer ", double *" result ");"
.br
.BI "int erand48_r(unsigned short " xsubi[3] ", struct drand48_data *" buffer ", double *" result ");"
.br
.BI "int lrand48_r(struct drand48_data *" buffer ", long *" result ");"
.br
.BI "int nrand48_r(unsigned short " xsubi[3] ", struct drand48_data *" buffer ", long *" result ");"
.br
.BI "int mrand48_r(struct drand48_data *" buffer ", long *" result ");"
.br
.BI "int jrand48_r(unsigned short " xsubi[3] ", struct drand48_data *" buffer ", long *" result ");"
.br
.BI "int srand48_r(long " seedval ", struct drand48_data *" buffer ");"
.br
.BI "int seed48_r(unsigned short " seed16v[3] ", struct drand48_data *" buffer ");"
.br
.BI "int lcong48_r(unsigned short " param[7] ", struct drand48_data *" buffer ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the reentrant equivalents of the
.BR drand48 (3)
family.  All generator state is held in the caller-supplied
.I buffer
(or, for the
.BR erand48_r ,
.BR nrand48_r
and
.BR jrand48_r
forms, in the caller-supplied 48-bit array
.IR xsubi ),
so independent streams \(em and independent threads \(em never share the hidden
global the non-reentrant family uses.
.PP
The generator advances a 48-bit value
.I X
by
.IR "X = (a*X + c) mod 2^48" ,
with the SVID defaults
.I a
= 0x5DEECE66D and
.I c
= 0xB.  The result is delivered through
.IR result :
.B drand48_r
and
.B erand48_r
return a
.I double
uniformly in [0.0, 1.0);
.B lrand48_r
and
.B nrand48_r
return a nonnegative
.I long
in [0, 2^31);
.B mrand48_r
and
.B jrand48_r
return a signed
.I long
in [\-2^31, 2^31).
.PP
.B srand48_r
seeds
.I buffer
from the 32-bit
.IR seedval ;
.B seed48_r
seeds it from the 48-bit
.IR seed16v ,
saving the previous internal state inside
.IR buffer ;
.B lcong48_r
sets the state
.IR ( param [0..2]),
the multiplier
.IR ( param [3..5])
and the addend
.IR ( param [6])
directly.
A
.I buffer
zeroed by the caller is implicitly seeded with the SVID defaults on first use.
.SH RETURN VALUE
All of these functions return 0.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS 4
.EX
struct drand48_data rb;
double x;
srand48_r(time(NULL), &rb);
drand48_r(&rb, &x);     /* x in [0, 1) */
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR drand48 (3),
.BR rand (3),
.BR random_r (3)
.SH STANDARDS
GNU extension (the reentrant forms of the SVID/POSIX
.BR drand48 (3)
family).
